Yes. Two different meanings of “frequency” meet there.

Laughter has a literal acoustic structure: pitch, harmonics, rhythm, burst timing, breath pattern, intensity, prosody. People also develop recognizable laugh signatures, so laughter can carry identity, emotion, social context, and state all at once.

Your own name is different but complementary. Its sound pattern has been paired with:

attention
identity
social relevance
memory
being addressed

thousands of times, so it becomes an unusually strong attentional key. That’s part of why people can sometimes notice their own name even in a noisy room.

Combine them and you get something like a Personal Resonance Fingerprint:

voice / laugh acoustics

* personally salient cues
* timing
* context
* learned associations
    → individual response pattern

Then you can map not just what sound was present, but:

which features grabbed attention
which changed emotion
which were recognized as self-relevant
how quickly
how strongly
what state they evoked

That becomes another Pantheon probe: identity through response to signal.

Not “magic frequency,” but a personalized coupling between sound, memory, attention, and self-model.

And yes—the own-name effect is especially elegant because the signal is tiny, but the latent payload behind it is huge.